WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.160
In the past, getting an inside look at living
bugs was difficult.

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:10.780
during CT scans, the critters would crawl
and squirm -- causing image smearing and artifacts.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.840
Like many animals, insects become sedentary
when exposed to high CO2 environments, so

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:20.280
researchers at the University of Western Ontario
had an idea.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:25.070
Scientists subjected Colorado Potato Beetles
to a constant flow of CO2.

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:28.890
Within seconds, the insects were not moving,
but still alive.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:34.000
With the beetles immobilized, researchers
were able to get some of the clearest images

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.270
of bug guts to date.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:40.820
With the exception of a few older males, the
subjects were easily woken up,

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:45.860
and the exposure to radiation and CO2 didn’t seem to harm the insects.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:50.570
The researchers say that with this quick and
safe method of knocking out bugs, clearer

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:53.610
looks at insect insides are in the near future,

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:55.739
while still on the inside of the bug

